# 16. Database Replication

Replication creates copies of data.

Common model:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    W[Writes] --> P[(Primary)]
    P --> R1[(Read Replica)]
    P --> R2[(Read Replica)]
    P --> R3[(Read Replica)]

    Q[Read Requests] --> R1
    Q --> R2
    Q --> R3
```

Benefits:

* Read scaling
* Availability
* Disaster recovery

Important issue:

> Replicas may lag behind the primary.

Therefore:

```text
Write → Primary
Read immediately → Primary
Later read → Replica
```

may produce different results.

---

# 17. Database Sharding

Sharding splits data across multiple database nodes.

Example:

```text
Users 1–1M       → Shard A
Users 1M–2M      → Shard B
Users 2M–3M      → Shard C
```

Or:

```text
hash(user_id) % N
```

### Benefits

* More storage capacity
* More write capacity
* Distributed workload

### Problems

* Cross-shard queries
* Rebalancing
* Hot shards
* Transactions
* Operational complexity

Sharding should usually come **after** simpler scaling techniques have been exhausted.

---

# 18. Vertical Partitioning

Vertical partitioning splits columns.

Instead of:

```text
Users
├── profile
├── authentication
├── billing
├── preferences
├── analytics
└── history
```

create:

```text
users
user_auth
user_billing
user_preferences
user_history
```

Useful when:

* Tables become very wide
* Certain columns are rarely accessed
* Different access patterns exist

