# 37. Circuit Breakers

Suppose:

```text
API → Payment Service
```

Payment service fails.

Without protection:

```text
1000 requests
↓
1000 failing calls
↓
More retries
↓
More load
↓
Payment service gets worse
↓
Everything gets worse
```

Circuit breaker:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    API[API] --> CB[Circuit Breaker]

    CB -->|Healthy| PAYMENT[Payment Service]
    CB -->|Failing| FALLBACK[Fallback]
```

States:

```text
CLOSED
  ↓
OPEN
  ↓
HALF-OPEN
  ↓
CLOSED
```

---

# 38. Retries + Backoff + Jitter

Never blindly retry immediately.

Bad:

```text
retry
retry
retry
retry
retry
```

Better:

```text
1s
2s
4s
8s
```

Add jitter:

```text
2s ± random
```

This prevents thousands of services from retrying simultaneously.

---

# 39. Dead Letter Queues

What happens when a message repeatedly fails?

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Q[Queue] --> W[Worker]

    W -->|Success| DONE[Completed]
    W -->|Failure| RETRY[Retry]

    RETRY --> W
    RETRY -->|Too many failures| DLQ[Dead Letter Queue]
```

DLQs are essential for production event processing.

---

# 40. Event-Driven Architecture

Instead of:

```text
Order Service
   ↓
Payment Service
   ↓
Email Service
   ↓
Analytics
```

publish an event:

```text
OrderCreated
```

Consumers independently react:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    O[Order Service] --> E[OrderCreated Event]

    E --> P[Payment]
    E --> N[Notifications]
    E --> A[Analytics]
    E --> F[Fulfilment]
```

Tools:

* Kafka
* Redpanda
* NATS
* Pulsar
* AWS EventBridge
* Azure Event Grid
* Google Pub/Sub

---

# 41. Eventual Consistency

Not every component needs immediate consistency.

Example:

```text
Payment database
      ↓
Event
      ↓
Analytics
```

Analytics may update:

```text
within seconds
```

rather than:

```text
within milliseconds
```

Ask:

> Does this business operation actually require strong consistency?

